member.summary:
            Disables auto-matching/auto-inclusion for this (S,T) pair — top-level semantics

Top-level semantics: This (S,T) pair undergoes no auto-matching (including auto-flattening); only properties explicitly declared or matched by name-default rules are mapped;
            differs in granularity from the attribute-side [MapNoAutoInclude] (property-level), so both coexist in parallel rather than being equivalent.
Attribute-channel equivalent: 
            — class-level annotation that populates the same underlying storage field (NoAutoInclude) as this terminal; semantic equivalence is item-by-item;
            runtime consumption occurs via RuntimeSnapshotStore.IsNoAutoMatch(Type, Type) (direction-agnostic).
Reverse derivation semantic difference: Reverse mapping is derived by flipping each forward explicit declaration individually — under this flag,
            reverse mapping covers only explicitly declared properties ([MapFrom] channel auto-matched properties also participate in reverse mapping, constituting a perceptible difference;
            annotating [MapNoAutoMatch] yields the same narrowing effect — only explicitly declared properties are flipped).

            Global mapping configuration snapshot emitted by the source generator — a pure primitive-constant carrier, AOT-safe (zero reflection).

Publicly exposed solely because generated code requires it as a parameter type (precedent: ;
            generated code resides in the consumer assembly and cannot access internal members of the core library).
[For generated code only] Manual registration bypassing the source generator violates the "single source of truth" principle;
            at runtime, this triggers  conflict detection (when configurations across multiple assemblies are inconsistent).
 is represented as an int (to avoid generated-code dependency on enum symbols);
            its value is validated at freeze time to ensure it lies in the range 0–3; out-of-range values throw ;
            Type is represented as a full-name (FullName) string (for exact-match semantics).

            Abstract base class for JSON serialization — users may inherit from this class to replace the default System.Text.Json implementation.

Usage: inherit from this class and override Serialize/Deserialize, then assign an instance to 
            // Example: replace with Newtonsoft.Json
            public class NewtonsoftJsonSerializer : JsonSerializerBase
            {
                public override string Serialize(object? obj) => Newtonsoft.Json.JsonConvert.SerializeObject(obj);
                public override object? Deserialize(string json, Type targetType) => Newtonsoft.Json.JsonConvert.DeserializeObject(json, targetType);
            }
            JsonSerializerBase.Current = new NewtonsoftJsonSerializer();

            Multi-source composition mapping — constructs a single DTO from multiple source objects.

The DTO must be annotated with [MapFrom(typeof(PrimarySourceType))]. The primary source object is used to instantiate the DTO, while other source objects update or supplement its fields.
Processing order rules: ① The primary source (the type declared in [MapFrom]) is always processed first to create the DTO;
            ② Other sources update the DTO sequentially in the order they appear in the input array;
            ③ For fields with identical names, later sources overwrite earlier ones.
            Users can control the final value of overlapping fields by adjusting the array order.
            Example usage: var dto = Mapper.Compose<OrderDetailDto>(order, customer, product);

Multi-source composition list mapping — Batch-composes a list of DTOs from a list of source object groups.
Each sources array corresponds to one DTO; the  is invoked for each composed DTO.
Example usage: var dtos = Mapper.ComposeList<OrderDetailDto>(d => d.DisplayName = "x", sourceGroups);

            (Same-type mapping) Maps an object of type  to a new object of the same type.
            Usage example: Map(source);
Copy behavior: This method uses a hybrid approach — value types and string properties are copied by value;
            reference-type properties of the same type undergo shallow copy (shared references; modifying nested objects in the copy affects the original);
            reference-type properties of different types undergo deep copy (new objects are created and recursively mapped).
            For a fully independent deep copy, use .

            (Cross-type mapping) Converts an object of type  into an object of type  and returns it.
            Usage example: Map<TIn,TOut>(source);
Copy behavior: Value types and string properties are copied by value;
            reference-type properties of different types undergo deep copy (new objects are created and recursively mapped);
            reference-type properties of the same type undergo shallow copy (shared references).
            This behavior matches mainstream mapping libraries such as AutoMapper, Mapperly, and Mapster.
            For a fully independent deep copy, use .

            (Cross-type in-place batch mapping) Fills a pre-allocated destination array element-by-element from the source collection, updating only mapped fields.

Reuse contract: The caller must pre-allocate  (with length ≥ sources.Count) and initialize each element (destination[i] != null). When reusing the same array across calls, unmapped fields retain values from previous iterations (C6); callers must manually clear them as needed.
            In reuse scenarios, zero allocations occur at the top-level object.
Null handling (order and semantics): Validation proceeds in order C1 → C8 → C2:
            ① If  is null, throws  (C1 — highest priority: violation of pre-allocation contract must be explicitly exposed);
            ② If  is null, returns immediately without any filling (C8 — empty source is treated as no-op, and does not exempt C1);
            ③ If destination.Length < sources.Count, throws  (C2 — prevents index-out-of-bounds; explicit failure).
            Per-element slot processing:
            ④ If a source element is null, the corresponding slot retains its original value (C4 — “update only when data is present”; avoids overwriting valid content retained from prior iterations);
            ⑤ If the destination slot is null, the slot is skipped (C5 — update requires an existing target object; skipping is permissive rather than throwing);
            ⑥ Only the first sources.Count slots are filled; excess slots retain their original values.
            Combined effect: Updates occur only for slots where both source and destination are non-null; all other slots retain their pre-call state — ensuring the destination array remains safe, stable, and predictable across repeated refreshes without unintended null writes or data erasure.
Copy behavior: Mapped properties are assigned in-place; nested reference-type properties instantiate new child objects per iteration (dst.Child = Map_...(src.Child), not in-place updates); zero allocations at the top level; non-zero allocations across the entire object graph.

Heterogeneous deep copy — Converts TSource to TTarget and ensures recursive creation of independent copies for reference-type properties of different types.
This internally delegates to , because heterogeneous mapping naturally performs deep copying (creating new objects and recursively mapping them) for reference-type properties of different types.
Note: TSource and TTarget must not be the same type; for homogeneous deep copy, use . Usage example: var entity = Mapper.DeepCopy<UserDto, UserEntity>(dto);

Log callback — when set, Trace output is also written to this callback, enabling integration with or Serilog.
Usage: MapTracer.LogCallback = msg => logger.LogDebug(msg);
The field uses volatile to ensure thread-safe reads of the delegate reference in multi-threaded scenarios.

A masking implementation registry — allows replacing built-in IMask implementations at application startup without modifying DTO attribute annotations.
Usage: MaskProvider.Override<MaskPhone>(new JapanPhoneMask());
Constraint: Override must be executed before the first mapping invocation of that Mask type (static field initialization prevents re-parsing thereafter).

NCMapper configuration options — supports both IOptions pattern and appsettings.json configuration.
Usage 1 (code-based configuration):
services.AddNCMapper(opts => { opts.ValidationEnabled = false; });
Usage 2 (appsettings.json):
"NCMapper": { "ValidationEnabled": false, "MaxRecursionDepth": 128, "DeepCopyStrategy": "ExpressionTree" }
services.AddNCMapper(configuration.GetSection("NCMapper"));

Registers the NCMapper IManager into the DI container and configures options via a callback. Usage: services.AddNCMapper(opts => { opts.ValidationEnabled = false; });
When reading from appsettings.json, first bind the configuration section to NCMapperOptions, then pass it in:
var opts = new NCMapperOptions(); config.GetSection("NCMapper").Bind(opts);
services.AddNCMapper(o => { o.ValidationEnabled = opts.ValidationEnabled; });

Registers the NCMapper IManager into the DI container and binds options from an section. Usage: services.AddNCMapper(configuration.GetSection("NCMapper"));
Supported binding keys: ValidationEnabled / TraceEnabled / MaxRecursionDepth / MaxInlineDepth / DeepCopyStrategy
Note: (TextWriter) cannot be bound from JSON; to customize it, use the overload and assign it programmatically.

